4:1119","promise:bwb_daily_pallets_2023-02-09:W8-BOR-212","ia:dontbelieveevery0000thub","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_040.bib.mrc:241843943:3336"],"title":"Don't believe everything you think","dewey_decimal_class":["294.3/420423"],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Includes bibliographical references."},"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"lccn":["2012012415"],"subjects":["Requisites","Enlightenment (Buddhism)","Buddhism","Spiritual life"],"subject_people":["Rgyal-sras Thogs-med Bzaṅ-po-dpal (1295-1369)"],"publish_country":"nyu","publish_date":"2012","by_statement":"Bhikshuni Thubten Chodron","works":[{"key":"/works/OL16592173W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"covers":[14397739],"ocaid":"dontbelieveevery0000thub","number_of_pages":245,"oclc_numbers":["776501884"],"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"\"It can be hard for those of us living in the twenty-first century to see how fourteenth-century Buddhist teachings still apply. When you?re trying to figure out which cell phone plan to buy or brooding about something someone wrote about you on Facebook, lines like ?While the enemy of your own anger is unsubdued, though you conquer external foes, they will only increase? can seem a little obscure. Thubten Chodron?s illuminating explication of Togmay Zangpo?s revered text, The Thirty-seven Practices of Bodhisattvas, doesn?t just explain its profound meaning; in dozens of passages she lets her students and colleagues share first-person stories of the ways that its teachings have changed their lives. Some bear witness to dramatic transformations?making friends with an enemy prisoner-of-war, finding peace after the murder of a loved one?while others tell of smaller lessons, like waiting for something to happen or coping with a minor injury.\"--Amazon."},"latest_revision":9,"revision":9,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2012-04-14T08:18:57.557326"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-05-23T04:43:05.961864"}}},"ISBN